+# Decision: How committed changes reach browsers
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit one event revision. Up to 800 staff browsers then need to see those revisions. A busy market averages two status changes per minute, with opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second.
+
+The way revisions travel from the server to browsers shapes three design nodes at once. The change store's read and publication model, the delivery component's whole reason to exist, and the browser's subscription and recovery behavior all follow from it, so reversing the choice later means redesigning all three.
+
+Every option considered here meets the project's stated goals: changes visible within 15 seconds, 800 simultaneous browsers, recovery after a 20-minute disconnection, and one authoritative revision per committed edit. The tenets permit both self-hosted and managed infrastructure, and event updates carry only stall IDs and operational statuses, so no option is ruled out on data-handling grounds. The decision therefore turns on what the project values beyond those goals.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminates an option. The weighted criteria are:
+
+- **Update latency — major.** How soon after a commit a connected browser shows the change. The 15-second goal is the floor; coordinators acting on a power failure or a closure benefit from seeing it as it happens.
+- **Independence from a realtime provider — major.** Whether the board's delivery path, browser code, and availability depend on one external provider's product and subscription model.
+- **Recurring cost — moderate.** Ongoing spend that scales with connections or messages, beyond the servers MarketBoard already runs.
+- **Operational burden — moderate.** How much stateful machinery MarketBoard must build, size, deploy, and debug itself, and how much of the 99.5 percent availability target rests on it.
+- **Visibility of connectivity failure — moderate.** Derives from the tenet *connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board*. How naturally the option tells a browser that it has stopped receiving changes.
+- **Load when nothing changes — minor.** Work the server does for connected browsers while the board is quiet, which is most of the time at two changes per minute.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Each browser asks the server every five seconds for revisions after the last one it applied. The change store answers a cursor query; delivery stays ordinary request/response behind the same HTTPS boundary as commands; the browser runs a timer loop. There are no long-lived connections and no server-side session state, so any server can answer any request, deployments drop nothing, and recovery after a disconnection is the same request the browser makes every five seconds anyway.
+
+- Update latency: **weak**. A change appears up to five seconds after commit, plus request time.
+- Independence from a realtime provider: **strong**. Uses only MarketBoard's own HTTPS service.
+- Recurring cost: **strong**. No new service; cost is request volume on existing servers.
+- Operational burden: **strong**. Stateless throughout; nothing new to size or monitor beyond request rate.
+- Visibility of connectivity failure: **strong**. A failed or late poll is an explicit failed request the browser can report.
+- Load when nothing changes: **weak**. 800 browsers produce 160 requests per second, nearly all returning no changes.
+
+Summary: the least machinery and the clearest failure signal, paid for with lag and constant empty reads.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+Each browser viewing an event holds a WebSocket connection to a delivery service MarketBoard runs. When a revision commits, the change store publishes it and delivery pushes it to every connection subscribed to that event. Because a push can be lost and a connection can die without either side being told, the revision sequence in the change store stays the record: a browser that reconnects, or sees a gap in revision numbers, asks for everything after its last applied revision.
+
+- Update latency: **strong**. Changes are pushed as they commit.
+- Independence from a realtime provider: **strong**. The protocol is a web standard and the service is MarketBoard's own.
+- Recurring cost: **strong**. No per-connection or per-message fee; cost is the servers holding 800 mostly idle connections.
+- Operational burden: **weak**. MarketBoard owns connection capacity, dead-connection detection, reconnect storms after a deployment or outage, reliable publication from store to delivery, and replay for catch-up.
+- Visibility of connectivity failure: **partial**. A cleanly closed socket is visible immediately, but a silently dead one looks like a quiet board until delivery and the browser actively detect it.
+- Load when nothing changes: **strong**. Idle connections cost memory and periodic liveness traffic, not queries.
+
+Summary: fast updates on infrastructure MarketBoard fully controls, paid for with connection and recovery work it must do itself.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+MarketBoard publishes each committed revision to a managed realtime provider, and browsers subscribe through the provider's client library. The provider holds the connections, scales them, and handles reconnection. MarketBoard's delivery component becomes a thin wrapper that publishes to the provider and issues subscription credentials; the change store must reconcile what it committed against what was actually published, since a failed publish would otherwise leave browsers silently behind.
+
+- Update latency: **strong**. Changes are pushed as they commit, through the provider.
+- Independence from a realtime provider: **weak**. Browser code adopts the provider's subscription model, and board freshness depends on the provider's availability and terms.
+- Recurring cost: **weak**. A standing fee that scales with connections and messages.
+- Operational burden: **partial**. Connection operations are the provider's, but publication reconciliation, credential issuance, and diagnosing faults across a service boundary remain.
+- Visibility of connectivity failure: **partial**. The provider's client reports connection state, but a revision that committed and was never published is invisible to the browser without reconciliation.
+- Load when nothing changes: **strong**. Idle connections are the provider's.
+
+Summary: the quickest route to low-latency updates with the least connection work, paid for with a recurring fee and dependence on one provider.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets.
+
+The two major criteria decide it. Polling is weak on update latency; the hosted service is weak on provider independence; self-hosted WebSockets is the only option strong on both, and it is also strong on recurring cost. Its one weak fit is operational burden, a moderate criterion, and that cost is accepted deliberately: MarketBoard takes on connection capacity and reconnect handling in exchange for fast updates that no external provider can reprice, change, or take down.
+
+The choice is taken partly against the grain of the tenet *connectivity failures must be visible*, where polling fits better. That tenet becomes an obligation rather than a property the transport provides for free: delivery must detect dead connections and browser sync must show the coordinator when the board is not connected.
+
+Committing to this obligates the three dependent nodes:
+
+- **Change store** keeps revisions replayable from any revision a browser may hold, and publishes every committed revision to delivery reliably.
+- **Delivery** manages socket sessions and connection capacity for 800 simultaneous browsers, and detects connections that have died without closing.
+- **Browser sync** reconnects, catches up from its last applied revision, and surfaces a lost connection.
+
+The scale that makes this acceptable is modest: 800 connections in a single region, with multi-region operation a non-goal for the pilot. The judgment is worth re-running if connection operations come to threaten the 99.5 percent availability target, if the board must serve many times more browsers or more than one region, or if fast updates stop mattering enough to justify the work, in which case polling is the option to revisit first.
